Asthma Action Plan Creation/Review
General Practice Other
Established asthma history, Assessment of inhaler technique, Identification of triggers, Review of symptom control

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-002
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of asthma with progressive wheeze and diminished response to short-acting bronchodilators.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented an established asthmatic experiencing a rapid increase in breathlessness, requiring salbutamol administration every two hours with failing efficacy. Urgent face-to-face assessment was necessary to evaluate respiratory compromise, correct inhaler technique, and escalate therapy to prevent acute respiratory failure overnight. Delaying until the next in-hours period was clinically unsafe due to the rapid trajectory of clinical deterioration.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "My chest is incredibly tight, I'm coughing constantly, and my blue puffer just isn't working anymore."
History of Present Illness: The patient has an established clinical history of reversible airway obstruction and asthma. Over the past 8 hours, there has been a steady progression of chest tightness and dry cough, evolving into an acute exacerbation. There is a current reliance on short-acting beta-agonists, with usage escalating to every two hours. A review of recent exacerbation frequency and severity indicates a sudden departure from baseline control. The patient notes significant diurnal variation in respiratory symptoms and reports night-time awakenings due to cough or wheeze over the last three nights. An identification of specific personal aeroallergen triggers points to significant dust exposure earlier today.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently limiting mobility strictly to the bedroom due to moderate breathlessness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient lives alone, raising the risk of unrecognized rapid clinical deterioration.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 108 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Moderate respiratory distress, speaking in short phrases, audible polyphonic wheeze.
Physical Exam: Respiratory: Tachypneic with a noticeably prolonged expiratory phase. Widespread polyphonic expiratory wheeze auscultated across all lung fields. Assessment confirms an established asthma history. Assessment of inhaler technique demonstrates poor coordination and suboptimal inspiratory effort. The identification of triggers (dust aeroallergen) and a review of symptom control verify an acute exacerbation. Documentation of current peak expiratory flow rate baseline shows a drop to 55% of the expected value. Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm, no murmurs.
Environmental Observations: Visible dust accumulation on bedroom surfaces; ambient temperature is cold.
Medication Reconciliation: Salbutamol 100mcg CFC-free inhaler accessed and verified; Fluticasone 250mcg verified but non-compliant.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Asthma Action Plan Creation/Review (Acute Exacerbation) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Bronchitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of purulent sputum production, negative fever, and no systemic infectious prodrome.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Gradual onset linked directly to aeroallergen exposure, bilateral wheeze, and no pleuritic chest pain or signs of deep vein thrombosis.

Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear lung bases without crackles, flat jugular venous pressure, and no peripheral edema.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient demonstrated acute physiological decline with tachypnea, tachycardia, and a dropping oxygen saturation down to 94% room air, triggered by aeroallergen exposure. Telephone triage accurately flagged the failure of baseline short-acting beta-agonist therapy. Face-to-face evaluation was urgently required to auscultate lung fields, mandate the use of a spacer device to correct poor inhaler technique, and initiate oral corticosteroids to arrest the active inflammatory cascade. Deferring this intervention until the next in-hours period posed a direct and unacceptable risk of progressive respiratory failure and status asthmaticus overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered Salbutamol via a large volume spacer (6 puffs stat) with immediate improvement in air entry. Prescribed oral Prednisolone 50mg daily for 5 days. Created a written, stepped-up Asthma Action Plan.
Positive Behavioral Change: To dramatically improve medication delivery, commit to using a spacer device for every administration of the metered-dose inhaler. Reinforce this behavior by storing the spacer physically attached to the inhaler on the bedside table. The immediate visual cue prompts correct usage, and the rapid relief of breathlessness serves as negative reinforcement, increasing future compliance with the spacer.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Valved holding chamber (spacer) provided from the clinical bag.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 48 hours to assess the clinical response to systemic corticosteroids.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 immediately if you become unable to speak in full sentences, if your lips turn blue, if the reliever medication provides zero relief after 10 minutes, or if your breathing becomes universally exhausting.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Expiratory wheeze and chest tightness are expected to improve significantly within 4 to 6 hours following oral corticosteroid administration. Failure to respond dictates immediate hospital escalation.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate emergency medical care if you require your reliever inhaler more frequently than every 3 hours overnight, or if your peak flow drops below 50% of your established personal best.
PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: Oral steroids are required to reduce the severe, deep airway inflammation that standard inhalers cannot currently penetrate. The spacer ensures the medication reaches the lungs rather than being swallowed.
Actionable Steps: Take the oral steroids tomorrow morning with breakfast. Always use the spacer with your salbutamol. Wash all bedding in hot water tomorrow to minimize dust mite load.

